1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,724
Four levels of arithmetic, and every number in it was built out

2
00:00:04,824 --> 00:00:09,932
of variances. Watch what happens when the gauge stops being centred.

3
00:00:10,016 --> 00:00:14,195
Here is the gauge, centred, inside the tolerance. And

4
00:00:14,295 --> 00:00:17,908
here are the two percentages Level 4 produced.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,389
Now slide the whole thing three microns high. The parts have not changed,

6
00:00:23,489 --> 00:00:28,052
the noise has not changed - only the level. Watch the numbers.

7
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:33,234
They did not move. Not approximately - not to four decimal places,

8
00:00:33,334 --> 00:00:38,889
because a standard deviation does not know where the readings sit. There

9
00:00:38,989 --> 00:00:42,738
is nowhere in either formula for a bias to enter.

10
00:00:44,733 --> 00:00:48,138
The percentages are blind, but a shop floor is

11
00:00:48,238 --> 00:00:52,025
not - because bias does something noise never does.

12
00:00:52,116 --> 00:00:56,954
With no bias, half the scrapped parts are too big and half are too

13
00:00:57,054 --> 00:01:01,966
small. Add a bias and it all goes one way. By three microns, ninety

14
00:01:02,066 --> 00:01:07,502
nine percent of the rejections are at the upper limit - which is something

15
00:01:07,602 --> 00:01:12,440
an operator notices in a morning, and no variance ratio ever will.

16
00:01:14,333 --> 00:01:17,600
So how do you find it? Not by measuring the part

17
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:21,668
again. Repeat a reading a thousand times and you learn the

18
00:01:21,768 --> 00:01:26,017
spread precisely and learn nothing at all about where it sits.

19
00:01:26,116 --> 00:01:29,372
It takes a reference - something whose size you

20
00:01:29,472 --> 00:01:33,656
already know. Measure that, and the question becomes whether

21
00:01:33,756 --> 00:01:37,440
an interval on the average error still contains zero.

22
00:01:37,533 --> 00:01:40,864
Take the readings one at a time. The interval

23
00:01:40,964 --> 00:01:44,753
starts wide enough to swallow zero, and it narrows.

24
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:50,446
Eight readings settle a three micron bias. Halving the bias you want

25
00:01:50,546 --> 00:01:55,017
to catch does not double the work, it roughly quadruples it -

26
00:01:55,117 --> 00:02:00,338
and chasing three tenths of a micron takes nearly five hundred readings

27
00:02:00,438 --> 00:02:04,910
of a master. Accuracy is expensive in a way precision is not.

28
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,307
The second way to be wrong is to be

29
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:12,436
right in the middle and wrong at the ends.

30
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:17,689
Turn the slope up. The gauge now reads high on the big parts and low

31
00:02:17,789 --> 00:02:22,558
on the small ones, which is a real defect with a real cause - a

32
00:02:22,658 --> 00:02:28,201
worn anvil, a fixture that does not locate the same way across the range.

33
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:32,800
And look at what happened to the percentage. It got better. The

34
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:37,109
gauge's error grows with the size of the part, so it widens

35
00:02:37,209 --> 00:02:42,295
the spread of the readings - which is the denominator. The instrument's

36
00:02:42,395 --> 00:02:46,896
own defect is counted as process variation and rewarded for it.

37
00:02:48,883 --> 00:02:52,636
The third way to be wrong is to have been right. Run

38
00:02:52,736 --> 00:02:56,415
the same check on a master once a month for a year.

39
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:02,860
Month one: the interval contains zero, the repeatability is what it always was,

40
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:07,440
and the R and R number is exactly what it was last time.

41
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:14,388
And here is what the gauge was actually doing. Six microns

42
00:03:14,488 --> 00:03:18,625
of drift - nearly three times the gauge's own sigma, and

43
00:03:18,725 --> 00:03:23,543
a fifth of the whole tolerance. Every single study was internally

44
00:03:23,643 --> 00:03:28,764
correct, because the bias is constant inside one afternoon. The drift

45
00:03:28,864 --> 00:03:33,304
only exists between studies, and only if somebody kept them.

46
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:37,291
So five levels in, the honest summary is this. R and R

47
00:03:37,391 --> 00:03:42,170
answers one question about a measurement system, and it is not the

48
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:47,492
question of whether the gauge is right. Which leaves one more assumption

49
00:03:47,592 --> 00:03:51,705
standing: that a reading is a number at all. Level six is

50
00:03:51,805 --> 00:03:54,588
about the gauge that says pass or fail.

